decisions:
  - id: d-maestro-not-persistent
    decision: Maestro model (subagent spawns) instead of persistent headless agent sessions
    rationale: Persistent agents bleed tokens via polling (600K/session). Maestro spawns one-shot subagents with enriched context — 30-40x cheaper (15-20K/session). Proven model, already works via Agent tool. Context growth and concurrent file edits become non-issues.
  - id: d-ambient-as-memory
    decision: Ambient system is the between-sessions memory layer, not the within-session collaboration engine
    rationale: 'Field testing showed real-time ambient nominations are noisy and expensive. Ambient is better as the persistence layer: what agents learned, what patterns they discovered, what''s pending. Live collaboration uses Symphony messages written by Maestro during orchestration.'
  - id: d-temp-file-hooks
    decision: Hook scripts must use temp files for JSON parsing, never echo pipe
    rationale: PostToolUse hook receives 8KB+ JSON (tool_response includes full file contents). echo corrupts escape sequences before jq sees them. Temp file + jq < file is the only reliable approach.
learnings:
  - Plugin cache keys on version number — same version means no re-fetch even if remote content changed. Must bump version to propagate fixes.
  - PostToolUse hooks in Claude Code plugins DO fire for Edit/Write tools, but echo piping large JSON through shell corrupts it. The hook was running, just silently failing on JSON parse.
  - Max-based attention scoring (Math.max of 4 dimensions) gives 1.0 whenever any single dimension fully matches — useless for ranking. Weighted scoring with primary/secondary weights creates actual gradient.
  - The ambient nomination model works architecturally but the signal-to-noise ratio at default thresholds is terrible. Deduplication, surfacing config, and graduated thresholds are essential for adoption.
  - Persistent headless agents are architecturally attractive but economically ruinous. The Maestro model (intelligent dispatcher with ephemeral subagents) achieves the same collaboration with 30-40x less token cost.
