body: '## Session Arc\n\nStarted with implementing the 6-phase ambient plan (nomination engine → MCP tools → data policy → hooks → context composition → university). Shipped all phases across v5.1.0–v5.3.0.\n\nField-tested in deus-backend with a second Opus instance writing a case study. Found 4 critical bugs:\n1. Post-write hook JSON corruption (echo pipe vs temp file)\n2. Attention scoring always returning 1.0 (max vs weighted)\n3. Nomination spam from rapid tool calls (needed dedup)\n4. Hook-emitted events invisible to nomination engine (needed catch-up processor)\n\nFixed all 4 in v5.3.1–v5.3.3. The field test revealed a deeper issue: ambient as a persistent multi-agent system bleeds tokens and is noisy. Pivoted the Conductor team collaboration spec from persistent headless agents to the Maestro model — the active Claude Code session orchestrates subagent spawns with enriched context.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- Phase 1: Visible team orchestration (skill/behavioral changes)\n- Phase 2: Conductor ThreadView + RosterView (Swift)\n- Phase 3: Agent bench/activate + CLI roster command\n- Phase 4: Cross-platform web display\n- Phase 5: Neverland validation (10-session learning measurement)'
